# ECG Basics (Qazi) — Version 2, 18 items, all with an ECG
Q = [
dict(img='k2to1', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="An 81-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after fainting while walking to his mailbox. For the past month he has felt exhausted with minimal exertion. He takes no rate-slowing medications. His pulse is 42/min and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Serum electrolyte concentrations are within the reference ranges. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes this patient's rhythm?",
choices=["2:1 atrioventricular block","Complete (third-degree) heart block","First-degree atrioventricular block","Junctional escape rhythm","Sinus bradycardia"], key=0,
exp="At first glance this looks like <b>sinus bradycardia</b>: a regular rhythm at ~42/min with a P before every QRS and a constant PR. This is Dr. Qazi's \"trick ECG\" (class ECG 5). An <b>extra P wave hides in each T wave</b>, identical to the conducted P, so the atrial rate is ~84/min and <b>every other P is not conducted</b> = <b>2:1 atrioventricular (AV) block</b>.<br><br>Because there are never two conducted P waves in a row, you <b>cannot tell Mobitz I from Mobitz II</b>. Let the history decide: an asymptomatic 25-year-old is probably Mobitz I (benign), but an <b>80-year-old with syncope or exhaustion is treated as Mobitz II → permanent pacemaker</b>.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In 2:1 AV block a non-conducted P wave hides in the T wave, so the rhythm mimics sinus bradycardia. The type cannot be determined from the ECG, and an elderly symptomatic patient is managed as Mobitz II with a pacemaker.",
wrong={1:"In complete heart block the PR interval varies randomly (AV dissociation). Here every conducted P has the <b>same</b> PR interval.",
2:"First-degree block means a long but constant PR with <b>every</b> P conducted. Here half of the P waves (those in the T waves) are not conducted.",
3:"A junctional rhythm has no normal preceding P waves (absent, or inverted and close to the QRS). Here upright P waves precede each QRS.",
4:"Sinus bradycardia would have one P per QRS. The hidden P in each T wave means the atria are firing at twice the ventricular rate."},
eli5="The top chambers are beating about 84 times a minute, but only every second beat gets through to the bottom chambers. The blocked beat hides inside the T wave, so it's easy to miss. In an old man who fainted, this needs a pacemaker."),

dict(img='Lchb', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 70-year-old man comes to the office because of increasing fatigue and breathlessness on exertion for 2 months. He takes no medications. His pulse is 40/min and blood pressure is 158/62 mm Hg. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following sets of findings best describes the P–P interval, R–R interval, and PR interval on this ECG?",
choices=["Irregular · irregular · progressively lengthening","Regular · irregular · constant","Regular · irregular · progressively lengthening","Regular · regular · constant but prolonged","Regular · regular · varying without a pattern"], key=4,
exp="The P waves march through at their own regular rate, and the QRS complexes march at a slower regular rate (a ventricular escape rhythm, ~40/min). The two have <b>no relationship</b>: P waves fall before, inside and after the QRS complexes. This is <b>AV dissociation</b> = <b>third-degree (complete) heart block</b>.<br><br>Dr. Qazi's rule is to measure the same three things for every AV block:<br>• 1st degree: P–P regular, R–R regular, PR long but constant.<br>• Mobitz I: P–P regular, R–R irregular, PR lengthens then a beat drops.<br>• Mobitz II: P–P regular, R–R irregular, PR constant with sudden drops.<br>• <b>3rd degree: P–P regular, R–R regular, PR all over the place.</b><br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Complete heart block shows regular P–P and regular R–R intervals with a PR that varies randomly (AV dissociation). The atrial rate is faster than the ventricular escape rate.",
wrong={0:"An irregular P–P interval does not fit any AV block, because in every AV block the P waves march out regularly. If the P waves don't march out, think of something else.",
1:"Regular P–P, irregular R–R and a constant PR describe <b>Mobitz II</b>, where a P suddenly fails to conduct. Here no P ever conducts.",
2:"Regular P–P, irregular R–R and a lengthening PR describe <b>Mobitz I (Wenckebach)</b>. Here the ventricular rhythm is regular.",
3:"A regular rhythm with a constant but prolonged PR is <b>first-degree</b> block, where every P conducts. Here P waves and QRS complexes are unrelated."},
eli5="The top and bottom chambers of his heart have stopped talking to each other. Each beats to its own drum: the top at a normal pace, the bottom slowly on its own backup pacemaker. So the gap between each P and the next QRS keeps changing at random."),

dict(img='Lfl21', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 57-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of palpitations that began suddenly 3 hours ago. Her pulse is 150/min and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely rhythm?",
choices=["Atrial fibrillation with rapid response","Atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Wandering atrial pacemaker"], key=1,
exp="A <b>regular narrow-complex rhythm at exactly ~150/min</b> that begins suddenly and doesn't vary is the classic disguise of <b>atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction</b>. The atria circle at ~300/min and the AV node lets every second flutter wave through. The \"<b>sawtooth</b>\" flutter waves are best seen in the <b>inferior leads (II, III, aVF)</b>, where one of every two flutter waves hides in the QRS or T wave.<br><br>Dr. Qazi calls flutter \"highly tested.\" Typical flutter is a re-entry circuit in the <b>right atrium around the cavotricuspid isthmus</b>, whereas atrial fibrillation starts around the pulmonary veins in the left atrium. Rule of thumb: when you see a rate of ~150, look hard for flutter waves.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A regular narrow-complex tachycardia at about 150/min should prompt a search for sawtooth flutter waves in II, III and aVF, because it is atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction.",
wrong={0:"Atrial fibrillation is <b>irregularly irregular</b> with no organized atrial activity. This rhythm is perfectly regular.",
2:"Multifocal atrial tachycardia is <b>irregular</b>, with at least three different P-wave shapes.",
3:"Sinus tachycardia has distinct upright P waves in II and a rate that drifts with activity and stress. Sudden onset, a fixed rate of 150 and a sawtooth baseline argue against it.",
4:"A wandering atrial pacemaker has at least three P-wave shapes at a rate <b>below 100</b>. It is irregular and slow, not a regular 150."},
eli5="The top chambers are spinning around a circuit about 300 times a minute, which makes a sawtooth line. The AV node only lets every second one through, giving a steady 150. A heart rate stuck at exactly 150 is a big hint for this rhythm."),

dict(img='Lafrvr', cap='12-lead ECG with lead II rhythm strip',
stem="A 76-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of breathlessness and a fluttering sensation in his chest for 2 days. He has hypertension. His pulse is 140/min and blood pressure is 132/84 mm Hg. His ECG is shown.{FIG}This patient's arrhythmia most commonly originates from which of the following sites?",
choices=["Atrioventricular node","Cavotricuspid isthmus of the right atrium","Pulmonary vein ostia in the left atrium","Sinoatrial node","Ventricular Purkinje fibers"], key=2,
exp="No P waves, an undulating baseline and an <b>irregularly irregular</b> ventricular rhythm = <b>atrial fibrillation</b>, here with a rapid ventricular response. Dr. Qazi: it is the arrhythmia you will see most, about 90% of rhythm problems in adult cardiology.<br><br>Most atrial fibrillation is triggered by ectopic foci in the muscle sleeves around the <b>pulmonary vein ostia in the left atrium</b> (hence pulmonary vein isolation by ablation). Contrast typical <b>atrial flutter</b>, a single macro-re-entry circuit around the <b>cavotricuspid isthmus</b> in the right atrium. Consequences: loss of the atrial kick (20–30% of output) and stasis in the left atrial appendage, leading to thrombus and embolic stroke.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Atrial fibrillation (irregularly irregular, no P waves) usually arises from triggers around the pulmonary veins in the left atrium. Typical flutter circles the cavotricuspid isthmus of the right atrium.",
wrong={0:"The AV node is the site of AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia, which is a <b>regular</b> narrow-complex tachycardia, often with no visible P waves.",
1:"The cavotricuspid isthmus is the circuit of typical <b>atrial flutter</b>, which gives regular sawtooth waves, not a chaotic baseline.",
3:"A sinoatrial node origin produces sinus rhythm or sinus tachycardia, with a normal upright P before every QRS.",
4:"A Purkinje or ventricular origin produces <b>wide</b> QRS complexes (ventricular tachycardia). These complexes are narrow."},
eli5="Spots of heart muscle where the lung veins join the left upper chamber start firing chaotically. The top chambers quiver instead of beating, so there are no P waves. The bottom chambers are hit randomly, making a totally irregular pulse."),

dict(img='kmat', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 71-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted because of a 3-day exacerbation with increased sputum and wheezing. Her pulse is 124/min, respirations are 26/min, and pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 86%. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely rhythm?",
choices=["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter with variable block","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Wandering atrial pacemaker"], key=2,
exp="An irregular narrow-complex rhythm with <b>three or more P-wave morphologies</b> (impulses from several atrial foci) and a rate of <b>100 or more</b> = <b>multifocal atrial tachycardia</b>. It is classically seen in <b>chronic obstructive pulmonary disease</b> with hypoxemia and atrial stretch. Treat the lung disease, not the rhythm.<br><br>It is one of Dr. Qazi's five irregular rhythms: atrial fibrillation, sinus arrhythmia, second-degree AV block, <b>wandering atrial pacemaker (≥3 P shapes, rate under 100)</b>, and <b>multifocal atrial tachycardia (same, but rate ≥100)</b>. The only difference between the last two is the rate.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Multifocal atrial tachycardia is an irregular rhythm with at least three P-wave shapes at a rate of 100 or more, typically in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Below 100 it is called a wandering atrial pacemaker.",
wrong={0:"Atrial fibrillation is the main look-alike, since both are irregular, but atrial fibrillation has <b>no discrete P waves</b>. Here a distinct P precedes every QRS.",
1:"Flutter with variable block is irregular too, but it shows uniform sawtooth flutter waves, not several different discrete P waves.",
3:"Sinus tachycardia has <b>one</b> P-wave shape and a constant PR, and it is regular.",
4:"A wandering atrial pacemaker has the same three or more P shapes, but the rate is <b>below 100</b>. Hers is 124."},
eli5="Her struggling lungs irritate several spots in the upper heart chambers, and each one fires in turn. Each spot makes a differently shaped P wave, and together they drive the heart fast and irregularly. Under 100 beats a minute it would have a different name: wandering atrial pacemaker."),

dict(img='ksinarr', cap='12-lead ECG recorded during quiet breathing',
stem="A 19-year-old college swimmer is seen for a preparticipation examination. She feels well and has no palpitations, chest pain, or fainting. Her pulse is 60/min and blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. Cardiac examination shows no abnormalities. On the bedside monitor, her heart rate rises slightly with each breath in and falls with each breath out. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the irregularity of this rhythm?",
choices=["Chaotic firing from pulmonary vein foci","Multiple competing atrial pacemaker sites","Premature beats from an ectopic atrial focus","Progressive fatigue of atrioventricular conduction","Respiratory variation in vagal tone"], key=4,
exp="Every beat is <b>sinus</b> (same upright P in II, constant PR, a P for every QRS), but the rate speeds up with inspiration and slows with expiration. This is <b>sinus arrhythmia</b>, one of the five irregular rhythms, and <b>normal</b>, especially in the young and fit.<br><br>Inspiration → vagal (parasympathetic) outflow to the <b>sinoatrial (SA) node</b> falls → the rate rises. Expiration → vagal tone returns → the rate slows. No treatment or further work-up is needed.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Sinus arrhythmia is a normal variant in young people: identical sinus P waves with an R–R interval that varies with respiration because vagal tone to the SA node changes with breathing.",
wrong={0:"Chaotic pulmonary vein firing is <b>atrial fibrillation</b>, with no P waves and an irregularly irregular rhythm unrelated to breathing.",
1:"Multiple competing atrial foci produce at least three <b>different</b> P-wave shapes (wandering atrial pacemaker or multifocal atrial tachycardia). Here every P is identical.",
2:"Premature atrial beats come early with an <b>abnormal</b> P-wave shape, then a pause. The variation here is gradual and cyclical with breathing.",
3:"Progressive AV nodal fatigue is <b>Mobitz I (Wenckebach)</b>: the PR lengthens until a beat drops. Her PR is constant and no beats drop."},
eli5="When she breathes in, the brake nerve on her heart's pacemaker eases off and the heart speeds up a little. When she breathes out, it slows again. That's healthy and common in young athletes."),

dict(img='klbbb', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 58-year-old man who received a heart transplant 6 years ago comes for a routine clinic visit. He feels well. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes this patient's conduction abnormalities?",
choices=["First-degree AV block and left bundle branch block","First-degree AV block and right bundle branch block","Mobitz I AV block and left bundle branch block","Mobitz II AV block and left bundle branch block","Normal AV conduction and left bundle branch block"], key=0,
exp="This is Dr. Qazi's class ECG 1. Run the system:<br>• <b>Rate</b>: 10 QRS × 6 = 60, a regular sinus rhythm.<br>• <b>PR</b>: more than one big box (over 200 ms), constant, no drops → <b>first-degree AV block</b>.<br>• <b>QRS</b>: over 3 small boxes (over 120 ms) with a left axis; V1–V3 mostly negative and I, aVL and V6 broad and notched → <b>left bundle branch block</b> (\"WiLLiaM\": W in V1, M in V6).<br><br>Read: <b>sinus rhythm at 60, first-degree AV block, left bundle branch block</b>. (Dr. Qazi also pointed out double P waves, because this patient had a transplanted heart.)<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Read each interval in turn. A long constant PR is first-degree block, and a QRS of 120 ms or more that is negative in V1–V3 with broad notched R waves in I, aVL and V6 is left bundle branch block.",
wrong={1:"Right bundle branch block gives an rSR′ (\"rabbit ears\") in V1–V2 and slurred S waves laterally, with a normal axis. Here V1 is mostly negative and V6 has a broad notched R.",
2:"Mobitz I needs a PR that lengthens progressively until a QRS drops. His PR is constant with no dropped beats.",
3:"Mobitz II needs sudden non-conducted P waves. Every P here conducts, and the rate is exactly 10 × 6 = 60.",
4:"The PR is more than one big box (over 200 ms), so AV conduction is not normal."},
eli5="Two separate delays are stacked here. The signal is slow to pass the AV node, which makes a long PR. Then it has to detour because the left wiring branch is blocked, which makes a wide QRS."),

dict(img='Ltrifas', cap='12-lead ECG with lead II rhythm strip',
stem="A 90-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after two fainting episodes in 1 week. She takes no medications and has never had a myocardial infarction. Her pulse is 64/min. Echocardiography shows normal wall thickness and no valve disease. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the axis on this ECG?",
choices=["Block of the left anterior fascicle","Block of the left posterior fascicle","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Old inferior myocardial infarction","Right bundle branch block alone"], key=0,
exp="This ECG shows three conduction problems: <b>right bundle branch block</b> (wide QRS, rSR′ in V1, slurred lateral S waves), <b>left axis deviation</b> (I positive, II/III/aVF negative), and a <b>long PR</b> (over one big box, 200 ms).<br><br>The lecture's classic trap: <b>right bundle branch block has a normal axis</b>, so a left axis needs a second explanation. Dr. Qazi's four causes of left axis: left ventricular hypertrophy, left bundle branch block, left anterior fascicular block, and old inferior infarction. Wall thickness is normal (not LVH), the QRS pattern is right bundle branch block (not left), and she has never had an infarction and there are no inferior Q waves (no old inferior infarct). That leaves <b>left anterior fascicular block</b>.<br><br>RBBB + LAFB + long PR has been called \"trifascicular\" block, meaning the only remaining fascicle may be conducting slowly. In a 90-year-old with syncope, a <b>permanent pacemaker</b> may be needed.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Right bundle branch block does not shift the axis. RBBB with left axis deviation and no other cause means an added left anterior fascicular block.",
wrong={1:"Left posterior fascicular block shifts the axis to the <b>right</b>. Right bundle branch block plus right axis = RBBB + left posterior fascicular block.",
2:"Left ventricular hypertrophy causes left axis with high voltages, but echocardiography shows normal wall thickness.",
3:"An old inferior infarct causes left axis through <b>Q waves</b> in II, III and aVF. There are none, and she has never had an infarction.",
4:"Right bundle branch block on its own has a <b>normal</b> axis. That is exactly why an extra explanation is needed."},
eli5="The heart's wiring has one right branch and a left branch that splits in two. Here the right branch is blocked and one of the two left sub-branches is also blocked, which swings the axis to the left. Only one wire is left, and it's slow."),

dict(img='klvh', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 67-year-old man with long-standing poorly controlled hypertension comes to the office because of shortness of breath with exertion for 3 months. His blood pressure is 178/96 mm Hg. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following findings on this ECG is the most specific for this patient's diagnosis?",
choices=["Left axis deviation","QRS duration of 100 ms","R wave in aVL greater than 11 mm","S in V2 plus R in V5 of 35 mm or more","ST depression with T inversion laterally"], key=2,
exp="This is class ECG 4: <b>left ventricular hypertrophy</b>. Dr. Qazi's criteria: <b>left axis is required</b>, plus either voltage criterion.<br>• <b>R in aVL over 11 mm → most specific.</b><br>• S (V1 or V2) + R (V5 or V6) ≥35 mm (Sokolow–Lyon) → <b>most sensitive</b>.<br>This ECG meets both, with a left axis. LVH matters because new heart failure plus LVH on the ECG starts the work-up. LVH also brings T inversion and ST depression (\"strain\") that can be mistaken for ischemia.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> For LVH on the ECG, an R wave in aVL over 11 mm is the most specific criterion and S(V1/V2) + R(V5/V6) ≥35 mm is the most sensitive. Dr. Qazi also requires a left axis.",
wrong={0:"Left axis is <b>required</b> for LVH in Dr. Qazi's scheme, but it is not specific: left bundle branch block, left anterior fascicular block and old inferior infarction also cause it.",
1:"A QRS of 100 ms is normal (under 120 ms). It rules out a bundle branch block but does not diagnose hypertrophy.",
3:"The Sokolow–Lyon sum is the <b>most sensitive</b> criterion, not the most specific. It is often met in thin young people with normal hearts.",
4:"Lateral \"strain\" ST–T changes accompany LVH, but they are nonspecific. Ischemia, digoxin and electrolyte problems produce similar changes."},
eli5="Years of high blood pressure thickened his left ventricle, so the ECG waves get extra tall. A tall wave in lead aVL is the most trustworthy sign. Adding the chest-lead waves catches more cases but is fooled more often."),

dict(img='Llqt', cap='12-lead ECG with lead II rhythm strip',
stem="A 24-year-old woman is referred after two episodes of fainting, one while she was startled by a telephone ringing at night. Her mother's sister died suddenly at age 30 years. She takes no medications. Serum potassium, magnesium, and calcium concentrations are within the reference ranges. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}This patient is at greatest risk for which of the following arrhythmias?",
choices=["Atrial fibrillation","Complete heart block","Mobitz type II atrioventricular block","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Sinus bradycardia"], key=3,
exp="The QT is markedly prolonged: the T wave ends <b>beyond the halfway point</b> between R waves (Dr. Qazi's eyeball test). With syncope on startle, normal electrolytes, no drugs and a family history of sudden death, this is <b>congenital long QT syndrome</b> (Romano–Ward, autosomal dominant).<br><br>Delayed repolarization → early afterdepolarizations → <b>polymorphic ventricular tachycardia = torsades de pointes</b> (\"twisting\" QRS amplitude) → syncope or sudden death. Normal corrected QT is under 450 ms (men) or 460 ms (women), and <b>over 500 ms</b> is where torsades risk climbs sharply. For a young person who collapses, the <b>12-lead is the key first test</b>.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A corrected QT over 500 ms greatly increases the risk of torsades de pointes (polymorphic ventricular tachycardia). Congenital long QT presents with syncope triggered by exertion, startle or noise.",
wrong={0:"Atrial fibrillation is common in older people with structural heart disease. It is not the arrhythmia that long QT causes.",
1:"Complete heart block is an AV conduction failure (seen with inferior infarction, Lyme disease or fibrosis). Long QT is a repolarization problem.",
2:"Mobitz II is a disease of the conduction system below the AV node. It is not triggered by delayed ventricular repolarization.",
4:"Sinus bradycardia may coexist and makes torsades more likely (pauses lengthen the QT), but it is not the dangerous arrhythmia that causes her syncope."},
eli5="Her heart cells take too long to reset after each beat, which shows as a stretched-out QT. During a scare the rhythm can twist into a dangerous fast pattern called torsades. That's why she faints, and why a relative may have died suddenly."),

dict(img='Ltdp', cap='Rhythm strip recorded during the episode',
stem="A 60-year-old man with ischemic heart disease and atrial fibrillation is found unresponsive on the telemetry ward; he regains consciousness after 20 seconds. He started dofetilide 3 days ago. He has had several days of diarrhea and takes omeprazole daily. The rhythm strip recorded during the episode is shown. Serum potassium concentration is 4.2 mEq/L, calcium is 9.3 mg/dL, and sodium is 138 mEq/L.{FIG}Which of the following additional laboratory abnormalities most likely contributed to this arrhythmia?",
choices=["Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypermagnesemia","Hypernatremia","Hypomagnesemia"], key=4,
exp="The strip shows <b>torsades de pointes</b>: a wide-complex polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS amplitude twists around the baseline, on a background of a <b>long QT</b>.<br><br>Stacked risks:<br>• <b>Dofetilide</b>, a class III antiarrhythmic and the one drug Dr. Qazi has personally seen cause torsades (hence in-hospital loading with QT checks).<br>• <b>Low magnesium</b>: diarrhea plus a proton pump inhibitor (omeprazole impairs magnesium absorption). His potassium is normal, so magnesium is the missing piece.<br><br>Low magnesium and low potassium both prolong the QT. <b>Intravenous magnesium sulfate</b> is the first-line treatment for torsades, even when the level is normal. Always check the medication list and electrolytes before prescribing QT-prolonging drugs.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Torsades de pointes is precipitated by QT-prolonging drugs (such as dofetilide) plus hypomagnesemia or hypokalemia. Treat with intravenous magnesium.",
wrong={0:"<b>High</b> calcium <b>shortens</b> the QT interval. Low calcium lengthens it, and his calcium is normal anyway.",
1:"High potassium widens the QRS and peaks the T waves, and it shortens rather than prolongs repolarization. Low potassium is the torsades risk, and his potassium is normal.",
2:"High magnesium slows conduction and can cause hypotension or heart block. It protects against torsades rather than causing it.",
3:"Sodium abnormalities cause neurologic symptoms and do not meaningfully prolong the QT."},
eli5="A heart-rhythm medicine plus low magnesium from diarrhea and a stomach pill made his heart cells slow to reset. The rhythm then twisted into torsades. Giving magnesium is the first fix."),

dict(img='Lhighk', cap='12-lead ECG with lead II rhythm strip',
stem="A 58-year-old man who receives hemodialysis three times weekly missed two sessions while on a fishing trip. He is brought to the emergency department because of profound weakness. His pulse is 96/min and blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?",
choices=["Serum calcium 13.2 mg/dL","Serum magnesium 1.0 mg/dL","Serum potassium 2.1 mEq/L","Serum potassium 4.4 mEq/L","Serum potassium 9.6 mEq/L"], key=4,
exp="A dialysis patient who misses sessions accumulates potassium. The ECG shows the progression of <b>severe hyperkalemia</b>: tall, narrow, <b>peaked (tented) T waves</b> first → then <b>small or absent P waves</b> and a lengthening PR → <b>widening QRS</b> → a sine-wave pattern → ventricular fibrillation or asystole. Dr. Qazi: \"high K prolongs everything.\" His potassium was 9.6 mEq/L.<br><br>This is an emergency: intravenous calcium to stabilize the membrane, then insulin with glucose (and β2-agonists) to shift potassium into cells, then removal (dialysis).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hyperkalemia progresses from peaked T waves to loss of P waves, a widening QRS and a sine-wave pattern. Missed dialysis is a classic cause.",
wrong={0:"Hypercalcemia <b>shortens</b> the QT (a short ST segment). It does not flatten P waves, widen the QRS or tent the T waves.",
1:"Hypomagnesemia prolongs the QT and predisposes to torsades. It does not produce tall tented T waves with a wide QRS.",
2:"Hypokalemia does the opposite: <b>flat</b> T waves and prominent <b>U waves</b>.",
3:"A potassium of 4.4 mEq/L is normal and would give a normal ECG. The changes here are dramatic."},
eli5="His kidneys don't work, so without dialysis potassium built up in his blood. Too much potassium makes the T waves tall and spiky and stretches every part of the heartbeat. It is a true emergency."),

dict(img='Lami', cap='12-lead ECG with lead II rhythm strip',
stem="A 63-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 2 hours of substernal chest pressure with sweating and nausea. Her pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 116/74 mm Hg. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Occlusion of which of the following arteries is most likely responsible for these findings?",
choices=["Left circumflex artery","Posterior descending artery","Proximal left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery","Second obtuse marginal branch of the circumflex"], key=2,
exp="ST elevation in <b>V1–V4</b> (anteroseptal and anterior walls) <b>plus I and aVL</b> (high lateral, fed by the diagonal branches) with reciprocal inferior ST depression = an <b>extensive anterior STEMI</b> from a <b>proximal left anterior descending (LAD) artery</b> occlusion, above the first diagonal branch. This is the \"widowmaker\".<br><br>Dr. Qazi's territory map:<br>• II, III, aVF → inferior → right coronary artery (~80%) or circumflex.<br>• V1–V3 anteroseptal, V4–V6 anterior, I and aVL anterolateral → <b>LAD</b>.<br>• Changes in nearly all these leads → proximal LAD.<br>Reciprocal ST depression in the opposite leads confirms a STEMI → open the artery now (\"time is muscle\").<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> ST elevation across V1–V4 plus I and aVL localizes to a proximal LAD occlusion. Reciprocal inferior ST depression confirms the STEMI.",
wrong={0:"Circumflex occlusion affects the lateral and posterior walls, giving changes in I, aVL and V5–V6, or ST depression in V1–V3 for a posterior infarct. It does not cause ST elevation in V1–V4.",
1:"The posterior descending artery supplies the <b>inferior</b> wall, giving ST elevation in II, III and aVF.",
3:"Right coronary occlusion causes an <b>inferior</b> STEMI (II, III, aVF), often with bradycardia. Here the inferior leads show ST <b>depression</b>, the mirror image.",
4:"An obtuse marginal branch supplies part of the lateral wall. Its occlusion would give limited lateral changes, not elevation across V1–V4."},
eli5="The ST-segment tombstones sit over the front of the heart and its upper side. Those areas are fed by the top of the LAD, the widowmaker artery. The dips in the bottom leads are just the mirror image."),

dict(img='Lpost', cap='12-lead ECG with lead II rhythm strip',
stem="A 60-year-old woman with diabetes mellitus comes to the emergency department because of 3 hours of chest pain radiating to her back. Her pulse is 92/min and blood pressure is 124/80 mm Hg. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}These findings are most consistent with acute injury to which of the following regions?",
choices=["Anteroseptal wall subendocardium","High lateral wall","Inferior wall","Posterior (inferolateral) wall, transmural","Right ventricle"], key=3,
exp="ST <b>depression</b> in V1–V3 is the trap. With upright T waves and a tall R wave in V2, it is the <b>mirror image</b> of a transmural injury on the <b>back</b> of the heart. No lead faces the posterior (inferolateral) wall directly, so V1–V3 see it \"from the front\" upside down. ST depression becomes elevation, and a Q wave becomes a tall R. This is an acute <b>posterior myocardial infarction</b>, usually from the <b>left circumflex</b> (or a right coronary) artery, and it is a STEMI equivalent (posterior leads V7–V9 confirm it).<br><br>Dr. Qazi's table: the circumflex supplies the posterior wall with \"no direct leads → reciprocal changes in V1–V3.\" Crib sheet: ST depression + tall R + upright T in V1–V3 = posterior MI.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> ST depression with tall R waves and upright T waves in V1–V3 is the reciprocal picture of an acute posterior (transmural) infarction, usually from the circumflex artery.",
wrong={0:"Subendocardial ischemia (NSTEMI) causes ST depression that is usually <b>diffuse</b>, often with T inversion, and it does not produce a tall R in V2. Localized V1–V3 depression with upright T waves points to the posterior wall.",
1:"High lateral injury (diagonal or circumflex branch) gives ST elevation in I and aVL.",
2:"Inferior injury gives ST <b>elevation</b> in II, III and aVF, which is absent here.",
4:"Right ventricular infarction accompanies an inferior STEMI and is seen with right-sided leads (V4R). It does not cause isolated V1–V3 depression."},
eli5="No ECG lead sits on the back of the heart. So an injury there shows up upside down in the front leads, as dips instead of bumps. Flip V1 to V3 over in your head and you'd see a heart attack."),

dict(img='Loldmi', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 53-year-old man with ischemic heart disease comes for a routine follow-up visit. He has had no chest pain for 2 years and exercises without symptoms. His pulse is 72/min. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the findings in the inferior leads?",
choices=["Acute transmural injury of the inferior wall","Completed infarction of the inferior wall","Normal variant seen only in lead III","Subendocardial ischemia of the inferior wall","Viral inflammation of the pericardium"], key=1,
exp="<b>Q waves</b> at least 1 small box wide and deep in an <b>anatomic pattern</b> (II, III, aVF = inferior) with <b>flat ST segments</b> mean <b>dead myocardium, an old completed inferior infarction</b>. The infarct is usually completed by 12–24 hours, and the Q waves persist for life.<br><br>Dr. Qazi's three findings:<br>• ST elevation = acute transmural injury (STEMI).<br>• ST depression = subendocardial injury (NSTEMI).<br>• Q waves = dead, old infarct.<br>A small isolated Q in lead III alone can be normal, but Q waves in III, aVF and II together are pathologic. An old inferior infarct is also one of the four causes of left axis deviation.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Pathologic Q waves (1 small box or more) in contiguous leads with a flat ST segment indicate a completed (old) infarction in that territory.",
wrong={0:"Acute transmural injury would show <b>ST elevation</b> in II, III and aVF with reciprocal changes. His ST segments are at baseline and he is asymptomatic.",
2:"A Q wave can be normal in <b>lead III alone</b>. Here there are Q waves in III, aVF and II, which is an anatomic (inferior) pattern.",
3:"Subendocardial ischemia shows as <b>ST depression</b>, not Q waves.",
4:"Pericarditis causes diffuse ST elevation with PR depression, not regional Q waves."},
eli5="Some time ago part of the bottom wall of his heart died from a heart attack and turned to scar. Scar doesn't conduct electricity, so those leads show a downward Q wave. With no ST changes, it's old damage, not a new attack."),

dict(img='knstemi', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 67-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 90 minutes of substernal chest pressure at rest. Her pulse is 92/min and blood pressure is 142/86 mm Hg. Serum troponin I concentration is increased and rises further on repeat testing. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the most likely state of the culprit coronary artery?",
choices=["Complete occlusion by thrombus on a ruptured plaque","Fixed stenosis with no plaque rupture or thrombus","Normal artery with transient vasospasm only","Ruptured plaque with 70–90% narrowing and some flow","Spontaneous dissection of the arterial wall"], key=3,
exp="ST <b>depression</b> (often diffuse, not one territory) with a rising troponin and no ST elevation = <b>non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI)</b>, a <b>subendocardial</b> injury.<br><br>Dr. Qazi's pathology: plaque rupture → thrombus narrows the artery <b>70–90%</b> but <b>some flow continues</b> → the subendocardium (last to be perfused, highest wall stress) becomes ischemic → ST depression. Contrast <b>STEMI</b>: plaque rupture → <b>100% occlusion</b> → transmural injury → ST elevation → open the artery now. For NSTEMI her teaching was to <b>stabilize first</b>, without emergency revascularization.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> NSTEMI (ST depression, rising troponin) reflects a ruptured plaque with a partially occlusive thrombus and some continued flow, causing subendocardial injury. STEMI reflects complete occlusion and transmural injury.",
wrong={0:"Complete occlusion on a ruptured plaque causes <b>transmural</b> injury and <b>ST elevation</b> in a territory, which is a STEMI.",
1:"A fixed stenosis without rupture causes <b>stable angina</b> with exertion, and troponin does not rise. Her pain came at rest with myocardial necrosis.",
2:"Vasospasm (variant angina) typically causes <b>transient ST elevation</b> during the episode, usually in younger patients at night.",
4:"Spontaneous coronary dissection is a rare cause of infarction, mainly in young women and around pregnancy. It is not the usual state behind an NSTEMI in an older woman."},
eli5="A cholesterol plaque in her heart artery cracked and a clot formed, but it didn't block the artery completely. Some blood still trickles through, so only the inner layer of heart muscle is starved. That shows up as ST dips rather than bumps."),

dict(img='Laflbbb', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 60-year-old woman with hypertension comes to the emergency department because of palpitations for 6 hours. She has a known left bundle branch block on prior ECGs. Her pulse is 150/min and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg; she is alert and comfortable. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes this rhythm?",
choices=["Atrial fibrillation with left bundle branch block","Atrial flutter with left bundle branch block","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia with left bundle branch block","Torsades de pointes"], key=0,
exp="A fast <b>wide-complex</b> rhythm always raises the fear of ventricular tachycardia. Here two quiet clues decide it:<br>• The R–R intervals are <b>irregularly irregular</b>, with no P waves → <b>atrial fibrillation</b>.<br>• The QRS has the <b>same left bundle branch block shape</b> as her baseline ECG (wide, negative in V1–V3) → the wide QRS is pre-existing aberrant conduction, not a ventricular origin.<br><br>Run the system: rate → rhythm (regular or irregular? which of the five irregular rhythms?) → then the QRS width. Atrial fibrillation is the arrhythmia you'll see most.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> An irregularly irregular wide-complex tachycardia with a QRS matching a known bundle branch block is atrial fibrillation with that block, not ventricular tachycardia.",
wrong={1:"Flutter would show regular sawtooth waves and usually a <b>regular</b> (or grouped) ventricular response, often at ~150. This rhythm is continuously irregular.",
2:"Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia is typically <b>regular</b>, and its QRS shape differs from the patient's baseline bundle branch block.",
3:"Sinus tachycardia would be regular, with a visible P before every QRS.",
4:"Torsades is polymorphic: the QRS amplitude twists and changes beat to beat, usually with a long QT and syncope. Her complexes are uniform."},
eli5="Her heart is racing and the beats look wide and scary. But the wide shape is her old wiring block, and the beats are totally irregular with no P waves. So it's atrial fibrillation passing through her known block, not a dangerous ventricular rhythm."),

dict(img='khyper', cap='Leads V2 and V3',
stem="A 55-year-old man comes to the emergency department 25 minutes after the sudden onset of crushing chest pain while shoveling snow. His pulse is 88/min and blood pressure is 146/90 mm Hg. Initial serum troponin concentration is within the reference range. Leads V2 and V3 of his ECG are shown.{FIG}If the occluded artery is not opened, which of the following ECG changes is most likely to appear next in these leads?",
choices=["Development of pathologic Q waves only","Flattening of the T waves with U waves","Horizontal ST-segment depression","Return to a normal ECG","ST-segment elevation"], key=4,
exp="Tall, broad, symmetric T waves minutes after sudden chest pain are <b>hyperacute T waves</b>, the <b>earliest</b> ECG sign of a coronary occlusion (here the left anterior descending artery, V2–V3). Troponin often hasn't risen yet at 25 minutes, so a normal first troponin never excludes infarction.<br><br>The STEMI evolution (crib sheet): <b>hyperacute T waves → ST elevation (minutes to hours) → Q waves + T inversion as the ST returns to baseline → variable resolution</b>. Dr. Qazi: ST elevation = transmural injury from 100% occlusion → open the artery now, because \"time is muscle.\"<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hyperacute T waves are the earliest ECG sign of acute coronary occlusion, followed by ST elevation, then Q waves and T-wave inversion.",
wrong={0:"Q waves mark <b>completed</b> necrosis and come later (usually by 12–24 hours), after the ST-elevation phase, not as the next step.",
1:"Flat T waves with U waves are the signature of <b>hypokalemia</b>, not coronary occlusion.",
2:"ST depression reflects subendocardial ischemia (NSTEMI) or a reciprocal change. With a total occlusion the next step in the facing leads is elevation.",
3:"A persistently occluded artery does not return to a normal ECG. Changes resolve only if flow is restored, and even then Q waves often remain."},
eli5="The very first ECG sign of a blocked heart artery is tall, puffy T waves. Next the ST segment lifts up, the classic heart-attack pattern. Later, Q waves mark dead muscle."),
]
